        "concatenation"
      ],
      "question": "What is the purpose of the join() method in Python when applied to strings?",
      "options": {
        "A": "It splits a string into a list of substrings",
        "B": "It checks if two strings are equal",
        "C": "It concatenates multiple strings with a specified separator",
        "D": "It converts a string to lowercase"
      },
      "correct": "C",
      "explanation": "separator.join(iterable) glues the strings of the iterable together and inserts separator between them, so \"-\".join([\"a\",\"b\"]) gives a-b. It does not split (that is split()) or compare strings, so C is correct.",
      "topicQuality": "specific"
    },
